UPDATE hotels SET
  article = $ART$<p><strong>Sofitel Winter Palace Luxor</strong> is the Victorian palace on the east bank corniche, and the old winter palace luxor hotel is the address most people picture when they imagine sleeping in Luxor rather than simply visiting it.</p>

<p>It faces the river, and across the river it faces the necropolis.</p>

<p>That is the whole argument for staying here rather than anywhere else in the city.</p>

<p>From the terrace and from the river facing rooms you are looking directly at the cliffs that hold the Valley of the Kings, in the light that changes every twenty minutes through the late afternoon.</p>

<p>Behind the building there are royal gardens, and in front of it, a short walk along the corniche, there is Luxor Temple.</p>

<h2>Why Stay at the Old Winter Palace Luxor Hotel?</h2>

<p>Because Luxor is a city where the distance between your bed and the sites decides how the days run.</p>

<p>Almost everything worth seeing on the east bank is within walking distance or a very short drive, and the west bank ferry and bridge crossings both start from this side.</p>

<p>Staying here means the early morning start for the Valley of the Kings, which is not optional in this city, begins with a ten minute transfer rather than a thirty minute one.</p>

<p>It also means the hours between the morning sites and the late afternoon ones, which are the hours nobody tells you about and which are unbearable outside in summer, are spent in a garden rather than in a lobby.</p>

<p>Who it does not suit is the traveller who wants a contemporary resort with a large pool complex and a spa as the centre of the stay.</p>

<p>This is a historic building doing what a historic building does, and part of what you are buying is the age of it.</p>

<h2>The Two Wings, and Why This Matters More Than Anything Else</h2>

<p>The property has a historic Winter Palace wing and a modern Pavilion wing, and guests book the wrong one constantly.</p>

<p>They are not two grades of the same room, they are two different buildings with two different reasons to exist, sharing gardens and an address.</p>

<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Wing</th><th>What it is</th><th>Who should book it</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Winter Palace</td><td>The original Victorian building, with the period proportions, the staircase and the terrace that the hotel is famous for</td><td>Anyone whose reason for choosing this hotel is the building itself</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pavilion</td><td>The modern wing in the grounds, built later, with contemporary rooms and the same gardens</td><td>Travellers who want the location and the gardens without the age, often at a lower rate</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>How to Avoid Booking the Wrong One</h3>

<p>Ask which wing, in writing, before you confirm.</p>

<p>A booking confirmation that names a room type without naming a building has told you nothing, and the room type names do not always make the wing obvious.</p>

<p>Then ask which side of the building the room faces, because a river facing room and a garden facing room in the same wing are different holidays.</p>

<p>If the Nile and the necropolis are the reason you chose Luxor, the river side is not a preference, it is the point.</p>

<p>The people who come away disappointed are almost always the ones who booked a modern room facing the gardens and were expecting a Victorian room facing the water.</p>

<h2>Against the Old Cataract in Aswan</h2>

<p>The honest comparison is with the other Victorian river palace in our portfolio, because most travellers end up choosing between them or doing both.</p>

<p>Aswan's historic hotel sits on a granite bluff above a wide, slow, island filled stretch of river, and its terrace at sunset is the single best sitting down hour in that town.</p>

<p>Luxor's sits flat on a busy corniche in a working city, looking at a wall of desert cliffs rather than at islands.</p>

<p>Aswan is the better view and the better evening, and Luxor is the better base.</p>

<p>Nothing in Aswan is within walking distance of anything the way Karnak, Luxor Temple and the west bank crossings are here.</p>

<p>Travellers doing the classic route usually stay in both, and the sequence that works is Luxor first for the density of the sites, then Aswan to stop moving.</p>

<h2>Rooms and Suites</h2>

<p>The rooms divide first by wing and then by outlook, and those two choices matter more than the category name on the booking.</p>

<p>In the historic wing the rooms carry the proportions of the original building, which means high ceilings and a sense of scale that no modern room reproduces.</p>

<p>In the Pavilion the rooms are contemporary and more predictable, which some travellers genuinely prefer after a week of old buildings.</p>

<p>The river facing rooms look across the Nile at the Theban hills, and the garden facing rooms look into the royal gardens behind the hotel.</p>

<h3>Which Room to Choose</h3>

<p>Historic wing, river facing, and settle that before you look at anything else.</p>

<p>If the historic wing is not available for your dates, a river facing room in the Pavilion is a better outcome than a garden facing room in the historic wing, because the view is the thing you will remember.</p>

<p>A higher floor is worth asking for on the river side, since the corniche road sits between the hotel and the water.</p>

<p>Families and anyone travelling with a lot of luggage often find the Pavilion easier to live in, and there is no shame in choosing it for that reason.</p>

<p>Light sleepers should say so at the time of booking, because a corniche facing room in any wing sits above a road that carries traffic into the evening.</p>

<p>The quieter rooms are the garden facing ones, which is the one argument on the other side of a decision we otherwise make in favour of the water.</p>

<h2>Grounds and Facilities</h2>

<p>The royal gardens behind the building are the part of this hotel that visitors underestimate.</p>

<p>They are large, mature and genuinely quiet, which in the centre of Luxor is not a small thing.</p>

<p>There is a pool in the grounds, a spa, restaurants and a bar, and the terrace at the front where the view is.</p>

<p>The gardens and the terrace are where this property earns its reputation, rather than in any facility you could find in a newer building.</p>

<p>The terrace itself is a working part of the hotel rather than an ornament, and it fills in the hour before sunset when the cliffs opposite turn from grey to orange.</p>

<p>Anyone staying on the garden side should plan to spend that hour out at the front rather than in the room.</p>

<h2>Practical Notes</h2>

<p>October to April is the season here, and it is not a marginal preference.</p>

<p>Luxor in July and August reaches temperatures that make the middle of the day unusable, and the west bank tombs are hotter still.</p>

<p>Sites open early, so the working pattern in this city is out at six, back by eleven, out again at four.</p>

<p>Two nights is the minimum that makes sense, three is the number that covers the east bank and the west bank without rushing either.</p>

<p>Luxor airport is close enough that a late afternoon arrival still leaves an evening, which is worth knowing when the alternative is losing a night to a Cairo connection.</p>

<p>We book the wing explicitly on every reservation here, because it is the one detail that decides whether a guest gets the hotel they thought they were booking.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Which wing should I book at the old winter palace luxor hotel?</h3>
<p>The historic Winter Palace wing if the building itself is your reason for choosing this hotel, and the Pavilion if you want the location and the gardens without the age.</p>
<p>Confirm the wing in writing before you book, because room type names do not always make the building obvious and this is the most common booking mistake here.</p>

<h3>Is a river facing room worth the difference?</h3>
<p>Yes, and it is the one upgrade here we would argue for without hesitation.</p>
<p>The river side looks across the Nile at the cliffs holding the Valley of the Kings, and a garden facing room in the same wing is a completely different stay.</p>

<h3>How far is the hotel from Luxor Temple and Karnak?</h3>
<p>Luxor Temple is a short walk along the corniche from the front of the hotel.</p>
<p>Karnak is a few minutes north by car, and the west bank crossings for the Valley of the Kings start from this side of the river, which is the practical reason to stay on the east bank at all.</p>

<h3>How does it compare with the historic hotel in Aswan?</h3>
<p>Aswan has the better view and the better evening, and Luxor has the better base.</p>
<p>The Aswan property sits above a wide island filled river and this one sits on a working corniche, but nothing in Aswan is walking distance from the sites the way Karnak and Luxor Temple are here.</p>

<h3>What are the best months to stay?</h3>
<p>October to April, and the margin is wide rather than fine.</p>
<p>July and August in Luxor reach temperatures that put the middle of the day out of use entirely, and the west bank tombs are hotter than the east bank city.</p>

<h3>How many nights should I stay in Luxor?</h3>
<p>Three nights is the number that covers the east bank and the west bank without rushing either of them.</p>
<p>Two is the workable minimum, and anything shorter turns the Valley of the Kings and Karnak into the same overloaded day.</p>$ART$,
